Consider the set $\mathcal{S}$, comprising the squares of positive integers. Recent research has revealed additional properties about $\mathcal{S}$, specifically that:

(1) $\mathcal{S}$ is equivalent to a non-square set in terms of growth rate;

(2) the sumset $\mathcal{S}+\mathcal{S}$ is a finite union of such sets;

(2) the intersection of $\mathcal{S}+\mathcal{S}$ with any infinite arithmetic progression is also a finite union of sets with non-square growth;

(3) the additive properties of the set $\mathcal{S}$ are limited to these statements.

(4) However, there exists a finite additive covering of the natural numbers, $\mathbb{N$, by the set $\mathcal{S}+\mathcal{A}$, where $\mathcal{A}$, a subset of positive integers, has a significantly smaller cardinality and growth rate.

Our research focuses on the set $\mathcal{A}$, specifically that it is an additive complement of the set $\mathcal{S}$.

(5) As such, the set $\mathcal{A}$ shares finite additive properties with the set $\mathbb{N$.

(6) Conversely, we demonstrate that the set $\mathcal{A}$ possesses infinitely many statements regarding its additive properties.

