Consider a parametric family of distributions defined by $f(x;\theta)$ where $\theta$ is a parameter from the set $\Omega$, and a random variable $R$ which acts as an indicator for selecting an observation. Let $N$ be an independent and identically distributed sequence of these random variables and let $N_n$ be the count of observations up to $n$ which have been selected. A parameter of interest is a function $g(\theta)$ derived from the original parameter $\theta$. Estimation of $g(\theta)$ is achieved by maximizing the following likelihood function:

 \begin{equation}
 \frac{L_n(\theta)}{[f_X(\theta)]^{g(\theta)}} = \sum_{i=1}^{n}
 \left\{
 \delta_{i} \log f(x_i;\theta) + (1-\delta_{i}) \log f_X(\theta)
 \right\},
 \end{equation}

  where the observed data $x_i$ is represented by $\delta_i$. This method of estimation was introduced by \cite{roberts2004} and its asymptotic properties are investigated, particularly its consistency and asymptotic normality. It is shown that this method of estimation can be seen as a special case of estimating the mean of a parameter in a mixture of distributions, which has applications in the study of sampled data.

